def section4_rest(R):
    B = []
    blk = R["q4_2b_blockiness"]["rows"]
    B.append(("h2", "4.2 Kuwahara blockiness and complexity"))
    B.append(("h3", "4.2a Why the hard arg-min causes blockiness"))
    B.append(("p", "The classical output at pixel $p$ is $\\hat I(p)=\\mu_{i^*(p)}(p)$ with $i^*(p)=\\arg\\min_i\\sigma^2_i(p)$: a *discontinuous* function of the data. In any region without a sharp edge (a smooth ramp, a curved boundary, texture) the four sub-window variances are nearly equal and differ only by noise or by tiny differences in curvature; which window wins is then decided by arbitrary small fluctuations, and it changes abruptly between neighbouring pixels. Because the winning window is an $m\\times m$ box whose mean is *constant* over a region shifted by $\\pm k$ pixels, the output is piecewise constant with patches of the size of the sub-window ($m=(N+1)/2$: 6x6 for $N=11$, 8x8 for $N=15$) and straight, axis-aligned jumps between patches: staircase edges on curved boundaries, plateaus on ramps. It is not blur (a selected window mean is not a smoothing of the transition: a clean step is reproduced exactly, tested), not ringing (a mean of a window never leaves the range of the window's data, so there is no overshoot): it is a *selection discontinuity* (a hard decision, a non-Lipschitz map from data to output) whose spatial scale grows with $N$. Figure 4.3 shows the selection map (window index) next to the output: in the flat regions the winning window changes in irregular blobs of roughly the sub-window size (the winner is decided by noise), along the disc boundary it forms a coherent band, and the patch boundaries of the classical output (visible as steps on the boundary and as patches on the ramp, Figures 4.2 and 4.4) lie where the index jumps."))
    B.append(("fig", "figures/q4_selection_map.png", "Left: index of the arg-min window (0..3 = TL, TR, BL, BR) over the circle-boundary crop (N = 11, noisy input): the winner flips in blobs. Middle: maximum softmax weight of the soft-Kuwahara (0.25 = uniform, 1 = hard selection). Right: the classical output.", 1.0))
    B.append(("h3", "4.2b De-blocking: Soft-Kuwahara"))
    B.append(("p", "**Modification.** Replace the arg-min by a softmax over the four sub-window variances,"))
    B.append(("eq", r"\hat I(p)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{4}w_i\,\mu_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{4}w_i},\qquad w_i=\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\sigma_i^2-\min_j\sigma_j^2}{\tau}\Big),\qquad \tau=\hat\sigma_{n}^{2}"))
    B.append(("p", "with $\\hat\\sigma_n$ the Immerkaer estimate of the noise from the noisy image ($\\tau\\to0$ gives the classical filter, $\\tau\\to\\infty$ the average of the four window means; both limits are tested). The sample variance of an $m^2$-sample flat window fluctuates by $\\sigma^2\\sqrt{2/(m^2-1)}$ around $\\sigma^2$, so with $\\tau=\\hat\\sigma^2$ the windows are weighted nearly equally in flat regions (no arbitrary winner) while at a strong edge the variance difference ($\\approx\\Delta I^2/4$) is much larger than $\\tau$ and the weights become one-hot (edge kept). At the low-contrast disc boundary of the test image ($|\\Delta I|=40$, $\\sigma=15$) the difference ($\\approx400$) is comparable to $\\tau\\approx225$, so the weights are only partly concentrated (maximum weight 0.5-0.6 along the boundary, Figure 4.3 middle, against 0.25-0.3 in flat areas): this is the regime in which the soft filter smooths the boundary slightly. This continuous weighting makes the output a smooth function of the data."))
    B.append(("p", "**Metrics for blockiness** (explicit; computed on the noisy input at $\\sigma=15$, averaged over 4 independent noise realisations, and on the *noise-free* image, where every non-zero value is pure filter artefact): (i) *TV excess*: mean absolute gradient (anisotropic total variation per pixel) of the output minus that of the clean image, on pixels farther than 8 px from any strong edge, so that true edges are excluded - plateau-and-jump structure raises TV; (ii) *ramp gradient irregularity*: standard deviation of the horizontal pixel difference over the ramp rectangle, whose clean value is a constant slope (a blocky output has plateaus of zero gradient separated by jumps, so this standard deviation grows: this is the boundary-gradient-discontinuity measure suggested in the assignment); (iii) *ramp RMS deviation* from the clean ramp (grey levels); (iv) *circle contour deviation*: RMS (px) of the difference between the sub-pixel contour radius $r(\\theta)$ of the output and that of the clean image (same ray-casting procedure on both, 720 rays; rays on which the disc is hidden by the ramp rectangle are excluded), which is sensitive to staircase displacement of the boundary. Table 4.5 lists them; Figure 4.4 shows before/after crops at identical window sizes."))
    rows = []
    GS = "ramp_gradient_std_(clean_ramp_slope_is_constant)"
    CD = "circle_contour_deviation_from_clean_px"
    for r in blk:
        N = r["N"]
        for nm, key, ck in (("classical", "noisy_classic", "clean_classic"), ("soft", "noisy_soft", "clean_soft"), ("bilateral", "noisy_bilateral", "clean_bilateral")):
            rows.append([N, nm, g(r[key]["TV_excess"], 3), f(r[key][GS], 3), f(r[key]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r[key][CD], 2), g(r[ck]["TV_excess"], 3), f(r[ck][GS], 3), f(r[ck]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r[ck][CD], 3)])
    ci = blk[0]["clean_input"]
    rows.append(["-", "clean image itself", "0", f(ci[GS], 3), "0", "0", "0", f(ci[GS], 3), "0", "0"])
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.5. Blockiness metrics. Left block: noisy input (sigma = 15, mean of 4 noise draws); right block: noise-free input (pure artefact of the filter). TV excess = mean |gradient| in smooth regions minus that of the clean image; ramp gradient std = std of the horizontal difference over the ramp (clean image: the quantisation of the 41-level ramp, value in the last rows); ramp RMS dev = grey levels; contour dev = px.", ["N", "filter", "TV excess (noisy)", "ramp grad std (noisy)", "ramp RMS dev (noisy)", "contour dev px (noisy)", "TV excess (clean in)", "ramp grad std (clean in)", "ramp RMS dev (clean in)", "contour dev px (clean in)"], rows, "rlrrrrrrrr"))
    B.append(("fig", "figures/q4_deblock.png", "Before/after at identical window sizes (rows N = 11 and 15): noisy crop, classical, soft; right column: the noise-free ramp (display range 66-104) processed by the classical filter (top half of each panel: irregular vertical steps, coarser for N = 15) and by the soft filter (bottom half: smooth). The classical boundary of the disc is jagged with block-shaped steps; the soft boundary is smooth but slightly displaced/blurred.", 1.0))
    r7 = [r for r in blk if r["N"] == 7][0]
    r11 = [r for r in blk if r["N"] == 11][0]
    r15 = [r for r in blk if r["N"] == 15][0]
    m11 = [m for m in R["q4_1a_matching"]["rows"] if m["N"] == 11][0]
    cc = lambda r, k: r[k][CD]
    rng = lambda lo, hi: lo if lo == hi else "%s-%s" % (lo, hi)
    B.append(("p", "**Reading the table.** *Noise-free ramp (pure artefact).* The classical filter's artefact grows with the window size, as the assignment states: ramp RMS deviation %s, %s, %s grey levels for $N=7,11,15$ and ramp gradient std %s, %s, %s (the clean ramp's own value, caused by the quantisation of the 41-level ramp, is %s); the soft filter gives %s, %s, %s grey levels and a gradient std of only %s, %s, %s, and the bilateral filter %s grey levels and %s. At $N=7$ the classical filter reproduces the clean ramp exactly (RMS deviation %s) and is more accurate than the soft filter (its symmetric averaging adds a small bias), so de-blocking pays off only for $N\\gtrsim11$; at $N=15$ the classical ramp deviation is %.1f times larger than the soft one and its gradient std (%s) exceeds that of the clean ramp (plateaus separated by jumps coarser than the quantisation steps). *Noise-free contour.* The disc boundary of the noise-free image is displaced by %s px by the classical filter and by %s px by the bilateral filter, but by %s, %s, %s px by the soft filter for $N=7,11,15$: the averaging rounds the boundary, the price of de-blocking (a staircase on the circle is not visible in the classical output of the clean image). The noise-free TV excess is below %s for every filter and does not discriminate. *Noisy input.* The classical filter displaces the contour more than the soft one (%s vs %s px at $N=7$, %s vs %s px at $N=15$) and its ramp deviation and ramp gradient std are higher, but these columns mix residual noise, over-smoothing and blockiness: the soft filter reduces the TV excess by a factor of about %.1f at $N=11$, largely because it smooths more (Table 4.1: flat-field variance ratio %.3f vs %.3f for the classical filter at the same $N$). The noise-free columns are therefore the clean evidence of reduced blockiness, and the soft filter's gain in PSNR/SSIM (Table 4.2) should not be attributed to de-blocking alone." % (
        f(r7["clean_classic"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r11["clean_classic"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r15["clean_classic"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r7["clean_classic"][GS], 3), f(r11["clean_classic"][GS], 3), f(r15["clean_classic"][GS], 3), f(ci[GS], 3),
        f(r7["clean_soft"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r11["clean_soft"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r15["clean_soft"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r7["clean_soft"][GS], 3), f(r11["clean_soft"][GS], 3), f(r15["clean_soft"][GS], 3),
        f(r11["clean_bilateral"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2), f(r11["clean_bilateral"][GS], 3),
        f(r7["clean_classic"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2),
        r15["clean_classic"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"] / max(r15["clean_soft"]["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 1e-9), f(r15["clean_classic"][GS], 3),
        rng(f(min(cc(r, "clean_classic") for r in blk), 2), f(max(cc(r, "clean_classic") for r in blk), 2)), rng(f(min(cc(r, "clean_bilateral") for r in blk), 2), f(max(cc(r, "clean_bilateral") for r in blk), 2)),
        f(cc(r7, "clean_soft"), 2), f(cc(r11, "clean_soft"), 2), f(cc(r15, "clean_soft"), 2),
        g(max(abs(r[ck]["TV_excess"]) for r in blk for ck in ("clean_classic", "clean_soft", "clean_bilateral")), 2),
        f(cc(r7, "noisy_classic"), 2), f(cc(r7, "noisy_soft"), 2), f(cc(r15, "noisy_classic"), 2), f(cc(r15, "noisy_soft"), 2),
        r11["noisy_classic"]["TV_excess"] / r11["noisy_soft"]["TV_excess"], m11["soft_kuwahara_flat_variance_ratio_(tau=sigma^2)"], m11["kuwahara_flat_variance_ratio_measured"])))
    rows = [[t["lambda_tau/sigma2"], f(t["PSNR"], 2), f(t["SSIM"], 4), g(t["TV_excess"], 3), f(t["circle_contour_deviation_from_clean_px"], 2), f(t["ramp_RMS_dev_from_clean"], 2)] for t in R["q4_2b_tau_sweep_N11"]]
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.6. Soft-Kuwahara temperature sweep at N = 11 (noisy input, tau = lambda sigma-hat^2); lambda -> 0 recovers the classical filter.", ["tau / sigma-hat^2", "PSNR", "SSIM", "TV excess", "contour dev (px)", "ramp RMS dev"], rows, "rrrrrr"))
    # 4.2c
    comp = R["q4_2c_complexity"]["ops_per_pixel"]
    B.append(("h3", "4.2c Per-pixel operation counts"))
    B.append(("p", "Counting conventions: additions include subtractions and accumulations; the spatial weights $w_s$ of the bilateral filter and all constants are precomputed (not counted); a window of $N\\times N$ with sub-window $m=(N+1)/2$. *Bilateral* (per neighbour: one subtraction, one squaring (multiplication), one scaling by $-1/2\\sigma_r^2$, one exponential, one product $w_sw_r$, one product $wI$, two accumulations): $3N^2$ additions, $4N^2$ multiplications, $N^2$ exponentials, 1 division. *Classical Kuwahara* with direct window sums (per sub-window: $m^2-1$ additions for the sum, $m^2$ multiplications and $m^2-1$ additions for the sum of squares, then the mean, its square and the variance: 3 multiplications, 1 subtraction): $4[2(m^2-1)+1]$ additions, $4(m^2+3)$ multiplications, 0 exponentials, 3 comparisons. With integral images of $I$ and $I^2$ (built once per image: 2 additions + 1 multiplication per pixel) each window sum costs 3 additions, so the count is independent of $N$ ($28$ additions, $14$ multiplications). *Soft-Kuwahara* adds to Kuwahara: 4 subtractions ($\\sigma_i^2-\\sigma^2_{\\min}$), 4 scalings by $1/\\tau$, **4 exponentials**, 4 products $w_i\\mu_i$, 6 additions, 1 division."))
    rows = []
    for c in comp:
        N = c["N"]
        for nm, key in (("bilateral", "bilateral_(2r+1)^2"), ("classical Kuwahara (direct sums)", "classical_kuwahara_direct"), ("classical Kuwahara (integral images)", "classical_kuwahara_integral"), ("soft-Kuwahara (direct sums)", "soft_kuwahara_direct"), ("soft-Kuwahara (integral images)", "soft_kuwahara_integral")):
            d = c[key]
            rows.append([N, nm, d["additions_subtractions"], d["multiplications"], d["exponentiations"], d.get("divisions", 0)])
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.7. Operations per pixel (N is the window size; bilateral window = N x N).", ["N", "algorithm", "additions", "multiplications", "exponentials", "divisions"], rows, "rlrrrr"))
    tm = R["q4_2c_complexity"]["timings_512x512"]
    rows = [[t["N"], f(t["bilateral_s"], 2), f(t["kuwahara_s"], 3), f(t["soft_kuwahara_s"], 3), f(t["bilateral_s"] / t["kuwahara_s"], 0)] for t in tm]
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.8. Measured wall time (s, median of 3, this machine, NumPy implementations on 512x512; bilateral with the matched sigma_s of Table 4.1 and window capped at 25x25; informative only - not part of any numerical comparison).", ["N", "bilateral (s)", "Kuwahara (s)", "soft-Kuwahara (s)", "bilateral / Kuwahara"], rows, "rrrrr"))
    B.append(("p", "**The bilateral filter is the most expensive per pixel**: its cost is $O(N^2)$ and each of the $N^2$ neighbours needs a transcendental function (the exponential, tens of cycles) plus 7 other operations, because the range weight depends on the data and cannot be precomputed, tabulated per offset or separated into 1-D passes. Kuwahara needs only means and variances, which are sums that can be obtained in $O(1)$ per pixel with integral images, independent of $N$, and has no exponentials; the soft modification adds just 4 exponentials per pixel (independent of $N$). The measured times agree in ordering (bilateral much slower than both Kuwahara variants, which are within a small factor of each other)."))
    return B


def section4_3(R):
    B = []
    q = R["q4_3_breakdown"]
    an = R["q4_3_analytic"]
    res = q["results"]
    B.append(("h2", "4.3 Quantitative breakdown analysis of the bilateral filter"))
    B.append(("p", "**Synthetic step-edge generator and experiment.** A 64x64 array is split evenly into two levels $I_1=60$ (left) and $I_2=160$ (right), $|\\Delta I|=100$, plus zero-mean Gaussian noise $N(0,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma=\\mathrm{NCR}\\cdot|\\Delta I|$; 30 values of $\\mathrm{NCR}=\\sigma/|\\Delta I|$ are log-spaced from 0.05 to 2.0, and **every NCR uses 40 independent noise realisations** (a different random state per NCR; no fixed noisy image). Filter: bilateral with $\\sigma_s\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and two rules for the range scale: **rule A** $\\sigma_r=2\\sigma_{noise}$ (the common 'tie $\\sigma_r$ to the noise' practice, used in 4.1) and **rule B** a fixed $\\sigma_r=|\\Delta I|/2$ independent of the noise. *Edge preservation metrics:* (1) **gradient retention** $G=\\max_x|\\bar p(x+1)-\\bar p(x)|/|\\Delta I|$ where $\\bar p$ is the filtered profile averaged over the central rows (8 px margin) and the 40 realisations (1 = the edge keeps its one-pixel step, ~$1/(\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\sigma_s)$ = %.2f for $\\sigma_s=2$ if smoothed like a Gaussian); (2) **edge-band PSNR**: PSNR (peak $|\\Delta I|$) between filtered and clean image over the 6-pixel band centred on the edge." % (1 / (math.sqrt(2 * math.pi) * 2))))
    prim = res["sigma_s=2,rule=A"]
    ns = prim["NCR*_retention_0.5"]
    B.append(("p", "**Definition of the critical threshold.** $\\mathrm{NCR}^*$ is the noise-to-contrast ratio at which the gradient retention falls through 0.5 (linear interpolation in $\\log\\mathrm{NCR}$); this is a *definition*, the curve is a smooth sigmoid and no sharp transition exists, so $\\mathrm{NCR}^*$ depends on the chosen retention level (the alternative definition 'halfway between full retention and the Gaussian-smoothing limit' is also reported). For the primary configuration ($\\sigma_s=2$, rule A) the measured **$\\mathrm{NCR}^*=%s$**." % f(ns, 3)))
    B.append(("fig", "figures/q4_breakdown.png", "Left: gradient retention vs NCR (log axis) for sigma_s = 1, 2, 3 (rule A), the expected-weight model (black dash-dot, sigma_s = 2), the empirical NCR$^*$ (red, annotated) and the analytic weight-ratio value 0.347 (blue). Middle: edge-band PSNR vs NCR for rules A and B and the unfiltered noisy input. Right: analytic range-weight ratio, with noise in the difference (solid) and noise-free (dashed).", 1.0))
    rows = []
    for k, v in res.items():
        rows.append([k, f(v["retention_at_NCR_0.05"], 3), "n/a" if v["NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(v["NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3), "n/a" if v["NCR*_retention_halfway_to_gaussian_limit"] is None else f(v["NCR*_retention_halfway_to_gaussian_limit"], 3), f(v["gaussian_limit_retention_(continuous)"], 3)])
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.9. Empirical critical thresholds (gradient retention crossing 0.5, and crossing the midpoint between 1 and the Gaussian-smoothing limit) for all six configurations. 'n/a' = the curve never crosses the level in 0.05 <= NCR <= 2.", ["configuration", "retention at NCR=0.05", "NCR$^*$ (retention = 0.5)", "NCR$^*$ (halfway to Gaussian limit)", "Gaussian-limit retention"], rows, "lrrrr"))
    rows = [[("%.4f" % r["NCR"]), f(r["gradient_retention"], 3), f(r["edge_band_PSNR_dB_(peak=dI)"], 2), f(r["edge_band_PSNR_noisy_dB"], 2)] for r in prim["rows"][::3]]
    B.append(("table", "Table 4.10. Breakdown curve for the primary configuration (sigma_s=2, sigma_r = 2 sigma; every third NCR value of the 30 shown; all 30 are in results/q4.json).", ["NCR", "gradient retention", "edge-band PSNR filtered (dB)", "edge-band PSNR noisy (dB)"], rows, "rrrr"))
    B.append(("h3", "4.3c Mathematical explanation"))
    B.append(("p", "**Mathematical prediction (exact, given the assumptions).** Two pixels $p,q$ on opposite sides of the edge have noisy values differing by $d=\\Delta I+n_q-n_p\\sim N(\\Delta I,2\\sigma^2)$; on the same side $d\\sim N(0,2\\sigma^2)$. For $w_r(d)=e^{-d^2/(2\\sigma_r^2)}$ and $d\\sim N(\\mu,s^2)$, $E[w_r]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sigma_r^2}{\\sigma_r^2+s^2}}\\exp\\!\\big(-\\frac{\\mu^2}{2(\\sigma_r^2+s^2)}\\big)$, so the **expected cross-edge to same-side weight ratio** is"))
    B.append(("eq", r"\rho=\frac{E[w_r]_{cross}}{E[w_r]_{same}}=\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta I^{2}}{2(\sigma_r^{2}+2\sigma^{2})}\Big)\ \xrightarrow{\ \sigma_r=2\sigma\ }\ \rho=\exp\!\Big(-\frac{1}{12\,\mathrm{NCR}^{2}}\Big)"))
    B.append(("p", "The *noise-free* range weight $w_r(\\Delta I)=e^{-\\Delta I^2/(2\\sigma_r^2)}=e^{-1/(8\\mathrm{NCR}^2)}$ (for $\\sigma_r=2\\sigma$) is the quantity the question refers to; the noise in the *difference of two noisy samples* adds $2\\sigma^2$ to the denominator and is why 12 appears instead of 8. A Monte-Carlo check with $2\\times10^6$ pairs agrees with the closed form: NCR = %s gives MC ratios %s against %s." % (", ".join(str(m["NCR"]) for m in an["monte_carlo_check"]), ", ".join(f(m["MC_ratio"], 4) for m in an["monte_carlo_check"]), ", ".join(f(m["closed_form_exp(-1/(12 NCR^2))"], 4) for m in an["monte_carlo_check"]))))
    B.append(("p", "**Mathematical prediction of a threshold.** The weight ratio reaches $1/2$ when $1/(12\\mathrm{NCR}^2)=\\ln2$, i.e. $\\mathrm{NCR}_{\\rho=1/2}=1/\\sqrt{12\\ln2}=%s$ (analytic; this is a statement about *weights*, not about measured edge retention). As $\\mathrm{NCR}\\to\\infty$, $\\rho\\to1$: the range kernel gives the same weight to neighbours across and on the same side of the edge and the bilateral filter degenerates to the Gaussian spatial filter, smoothing across the edge; as $\\mathrm{NCR}\\to0$, $\\rho\\to0$ and the edge is perfectly preserved." % f(an["rho_half_NCR_(closed_form)"], 4)))
    B.append(("p", "**Heuristic and empirical observations (to be kept apart from the prediction).** *Heuristic:* an expected-weight model replaces the random weights by their means: for a pixel at column $x$ relative to the edge, $\\hat p(x)=\\sum_jw_{s,j}\\bar w_j\\mu_j/\\sum_jw_{s,j}\\bar w_j$ with $\\bar w_j$ the expected range weight above (the centre pixel has weight exactly 1). It ignores that the weights are random and correlated through the centre pixel's own noise. It predicts a retention-0.5 crossing at $\\mathrm{NCR}=%s$ (black curve of Figure 4.5). *Empirical:* the measured retention-0.5 crossing for $\\sigma_s=2$, rule A is **%s** (and %s for $\\sigma_s=1$, %s for $\\sigma_s=3$; rule B: %s, %s, %s for $\\sigma_s=1,2,3$)." % (
        "n/a" if an["model_NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(an["model_NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3), f(ns, 3),
        "n/a" if res["sigma_s=1,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(res["sigma_s=1,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3), "n/a" if res["sigma_s=3,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(res["sigma_s=3,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3),
        *["n/a" if res["sigma_s=%g,rule=B" % s]["NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(res["sigma_s=%g,rule=B" % s]["NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3) for s in (1.0, 2.0, 3.0)])))
    rows_p = prim["rows"]
    mrows = an["expected_weight_model_sigma_s=2_ruleA"]
    near = lambda rows, key, x: min(rows, key=lambda r: abs(r["NCR"] - x))
    emp_at_half = near(rows_p, "gradient_retention", an["rho_half_NCR_(closed_form)"])["gradient_retention"]
    mod_at_half = near(mrows, "model_retention", an["rho_half_NCR_(closed_form)"])["model_retention"]
    B.append(("p", "**Why the analytic weight-ratio value and the measured breakdown threshold differ.** (1) *They are different quantities.* $\\rho=1/2$ is a condition on the expected weights of a *pair* of pixels; retention is a property of a weighted *average* over a window, and the level 0.5 is itself arbitrary. (2) *Window geometry alone does not explain the gap.* The expected-weight model already contains the geometry (how many neighbours lie across the edge for a pixel adjacent to it) and it predicts a retention-0.5 crossing at $\\mathrm{NCR}=%s$, almost the weight half-point %s, while the measured crossing is %s: at NCR = %s the model gives retention %s but the measurement %s. The measured filter therefore preserves the edge *better* than the mean-weight model predicts. (3) What the model neglects is the randomness of the weights, and this is the likely cause: the filter weights are functions of the *actual* noisy values, so for a pixel next to the edge the neighbours on the other side whose noisy value happens to lie close to the centre value receive a large weight and the others almost none; the filtered value is a ratio of random sums, whose expectation is not the ratio of the expected sums (Jensen), and the centre pixel's own noise is shared by all its weights and keeps the pixel on its own side of the step (the weights favour neighbours similar to it, i.e. noise copying). This cause was not isolated by a separate experiment (the Monte-Carlo check above verifies the pair-weight ratio, not the retention), so it is a heuristic explanation. (4) The threshold depends on the filter and the metric: %s for $\\sigma_s=2$, %s for $\\sigma_s=3$ (a larger window gives more cross-edge neighbours), no crossing at all for $\\sigma_s=1$ (whose Gaussian-smoothing limit of retention is 0.40 < 0.5, so the midpoint definition applies: %s), and none for rule B (fixed $\\sigma_r=|\\Delta I|/2$ never reaches retention 0.5 in the explored range because its range kernel is independent of the noise). So neither number is 'the' threshold: 0.347 is the analytic weight-ratio half-point and %s is the empirical NCR at which the edge-gradient retention of this configuration crosses 0.5. The rule of thumb that follows is that bilateral edge preservation degrades progressively as $\\sigma/|\\Delta I|$ grows through 0.3-0.6 and is largely lost for $\\mathrm{NCR}\\gtrsim1$ (retention at NCR = 1 is %s, against 0.20 for pure Gaussian smoothing with $\\sigma_s=2$)." % (
        f(an["model_NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3), f(an["rho_half_NCR_(closed_form)"], 3), f(ns, 3), f(an["rho_half_NCR_(closed_form)"], 3), f(mod_at_half, 2), f(emp_at_half, 2),
        f(ns, 3), "n/a" if res["sigma_s=3,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"] is None else f(res["sigma_s=3,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_0.5"], 3), f(res["sigma_s=1,rule=A"]["NCR*_retention_halfway_to_gaussian_limit"], 3), f(ns, 3), f(near(rows_p, "gradient_retention", 1.0)["gradient_retention"], 3))))
    B.append(("fig", "figures/q4_step_examples.png", "Synthetic step edge at NCR = 0.1, 0.3, 0.6, 1.2 (top) and the bilateral output (sigma_s = 2, sigma_r = 2 sigma, bottom): the edge is sharp at low NCR and disappears into the noise at high NCR.", 0.85))
    return B


def section4_all(R):
    return section4_rest(R) + section4_3(R)
